Now create the `pathland_core::tokens` module with the reference resolution algorithm and the conformance-table tests.

Design:
```rust
//! Design-token resolution (spec/TOKENS.md) — the reference implementation.
//!
//! Renderers own scheme detection and default values, but the resolution
//! algorithm (dark-layer → override → default → parent-fallback → fallback,
//! plus the generative `space.<N>` family) is protocol-level and conformance-
//! tested here. Pure functions over `BTreeMap`s, `no_std` + `alloc`.

use alloc::collections::BTreeMap;
use alloc::string::{String, ToString};
use alloc::vec::Vec;

/// A resolved token value.
#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq)]
pub enum TokenValue {
    Color(u32),   // 0xAARRGGBB
    F32(f32),
    U32(u32),
    U8(u8),
    I32(i32),
    Str(String),
}

impl TokenValue {
    /// The wire `value_type` for this value.
    pub fn value_type(&self) -> u8 {
        match self {
            TokenValue::Color(_) => crate::value_type::COLOR,
            TokenValue::F32(_) => crate::value_type::F32,
            TokenValue::U32(_) => crate::value_type::U32,
            TokenValue::U8(_) => crate::value_type::U8,
            TokenValue::I32(_) => crate::value_type::I32,
            TokenValue::Str(_) => crate::value_type::STRING,
        }
    }
}
```

Wait — value_type::STRING is 0x05, and the token value on the wire for STRING is an arena ref (not the string). So `value_type()` is about the *declared* value type. For resolution, a Str resolves to a String. OK.

Scheme:
```rust
#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub enum Scheme { Light, Dark }
```

Overrides & defaults: `BTreeMap<String, TokenValue>` keyed by full path. For `dark.*`, keyed by bare path in the `dark` map. Actually the spec resolution walks: dark layer candidates `dark.path`, `dark.parent(path)`, ... `dark.root`, then base layer `path`, `parent(path)`, ... `root`. So:
- overrides: base map keyed by path; dark map keyed by bare path.
- defaults: base map keyed by path; dark map keyed by bare path.

Resolution function:
```rust
pub fn resolve_token(
    path: &str,
    scheme: Scheme,
    overrides: &BTreeMap<String, TokenValue>,
    dark_overrides: &BTreeMap<String, TokenValue>,
    defaults: &BTreeMap<String, TokenValue>,
    dark_defaults: &BTreeMap<String, TokenValue>,
) -> Option<TokenValue> {
    // Generative space family: resolve space.base (scheme-aware) and multiply.
    if let Some(rest) = path.strip_prefix("space.") {
        if let Ok(n) = rest.parse::<f32>() {
            if let Some(base) = resolve_token("space.base", scheme, overrides, dark_overrides, defaults, dark_defaults) {
                let base = base.as_f32()?;
                return Some(TokenValue::F32(base * n));
            }
        }
    }
    let mut layers: Vec<Vec<&str>> = Vec::new();
    let mut chain = parent_chain(path);
    if scheme == Scheme::Dark {
        // dark layer: dark.path, dark.parent, ... dark.<root's parent>? 
        ...
    }
    ...
}
```

Wait the spec: `layers += [dark.path, dark.parent(path), …dark.root]` — the dark layer is keyed by bare path (dark. prefix stripped). And then base layer `[path, parent(path), …root]`. So for path "color.primary":
- dark layer candidates (if dark scheme): "color.primary", then parent... "color.primary" parent is "color"? The parent-chain: the spec's parent fallback: "font.body.size" → override "font.body" wins. So parent chain walks stripping the last segment: "color.primary" → "color" → "" (root). "dark.root" would be "dark." hmm. The root in dark is "dark" itself? The spec says `dark.path, dark.parent(path), …dark.root`. dark.root = "dark" (the bare root is ""). So candidates keyed: bare paths.

So algorithm:
```
function resolveToken(tokenPath, scheme):
    layers = []
    if scheme == dark:
        layers += [dark.path, dark.parent(path), …dark.root]   # dark.* layer, keys are bare
    layers += [path, parent(path), …root]                       # base layer
    for candidate in layers:
        if appOverrides.has(candidate): return appOverrides[candidate]
    for candidate in layers:
        if rendererDefaults.has(candidate): return rendererDefaults[candidate]
    return fallbackValue(path)
```

Wait, the ordering: for dark scheme, the layers list is dark-first then base. So the override search iterates dark candidates then base candidates. That matches "dark.path override wins (dark layer)", and "base override falls through when the dark layer is empty". Good.

But the loop structure: first loop checks appOverrides for all candidates (dark then base), second loop checks defaults for all candidates. So a base override is found in the first loop only after all dark candidates miss. Correct.

Parent chain: parent of "font.body.size" is "font.body", parent of "font.body" is "font", parent of "font" is "" (root). The root candidate "" represents... the spec's "…dark.root" implies the root (empty). I'll include "" as final candidate but it won't match anything (no token named ""). Actually including "" as a candidate in overrides lookup is harmless (no override keyed ""). I'll include it for spec fidelity.

Generative space: `space.<N>` — resolve `space.base` through the same algorithm, then multiply. If space.base resolution returns F32. If space.base returns something else (e.g. overridden to a non-F32), fall back to... spec says "generative base falls back to renderer defaults". Since we resolve space.base through the algorithm which already falls back to defaults, that's handled. If resolution yields a non-F32, return None (renderer treats as missing → fallback). Actually the conformance table: "space.2 | either, space.base not overridden | renderer default × 2". Good.

Parent-chain function:
```rust
fn parent_chain(path: &str) -> alloc::vec::Vec<&str> {
    let mut chain = Vec::new();
    chain.push(path);
    let mut cur = path;
    while let Some(idx) = cur.rfind('.') {
        cur = &cur[..idx];
        chain.push(cur);
    }
    chain.push(""); // root
    chain
}
