Now I have the full picture. Let me formulate the plan with the new design.

The new model:
- `Theme` = a set of base (light) token overrides; all builders define ONE value: `color(path, argb)`, `f32(path, value)`, `u32`, `u8`, `string(path, value)`. No dark variants on Theme.
- Remove from Java: `darkColor`, `darkF32`, `darkString`.
- `AdaptiveTheme(Theme light, Theme dark)` — emits light as-is + dark with `dark.` prefix. Has `emit(OpcodeSink)` / `emitInto(OpcodeSink)`.
- Emitter integration.
- Rust mirror: remove `dark_color`/`dark_f32`/`dark_string`; add `AdaptiveTheme`; `Engine` gains `apply_adaptive_theme` (light + dark-prefixed) OR an enum. Keep `apply_theme` for a single light theme.

Let me now write the plan. I'll ask the two clarifying questions (Emitter API shape, Rust mirror scope).

Actually, let me reconsider the Rust design more concretely since the user already said "mirror in Rust too" last time. Under the new model:

Rust `Theme`:
- Remove `dark_color`, `dark_f32`, `dark_string`, and the internal `push_dark`.
- Keep `color`, `f32`, `u32`, `u8`, `string`, `override_token`.
- Keep `iter()`.

Rust `AdaptiveTheme`:
```rust
pub struct AdaptiveTheme {
    light: Theme,
    dark: Theme,
}
impl AdaptiveTheme {
    pub fn new(light: Theme, dark: Theme) -> Self { ... }
    pub fn light(&self) -> &Theme { &self.light }
    pub fn dark(&self) -> &Theme { &self.dark }
}
```
Engine:
```rust
pub fn apply_adaptive_theme(&mut self, adaptive: &AdaptiveTheme, guest) -> Result<()> {
    self.apply_theme(&adaptive.light, guest)?;
    for (path, value) in adaptive.dark.iter() {
        let dark_path = if path.starts_with("dark.") { path.to_string() } else { format!("dark.{path}") };
        ... emit SET_DESIGN_TOKEN(dark_path, ...)
    }
    Ok(())
}
```
Hmm — `apply_theme` currently handles Str via alloc_str. I'd refactor: an internal `emit_override(path, value, guest)` used by both `apply_theme` (light) and `apply_adaptive_theme` (dark-prefixed). 

Alternatively, expose `Theme::iter()` already public — good. AdaptiveTheme just iterates both.

Should the Rust `Theme` also expose `dark_prefix`? Or `AdaptiveTheme` handles it. Fine.

Now, for Java, the AdaptiveTheme:
```java
public final class AdaptiveTheme {
    private final Theme light;
    private final Theme dark;
    public AdaptiveTheme(Theme light, Theme dark) { ... }
    public void emit(OpcodeSink sink) { sink.beginFrame(); emitInto(sink); sink.endFrame(); }
    public void emitInto(OpcodeSink sink) {
        light.emitInto(sink);
        for each dark override: sink.setDesignToken(dark(path), valueType, value);
    }
}
```
Need access to Theme's overrides. Options:
- Add package-private `Theme` methods: `void forEachOverride(...)` or a package-private accessor. I'll add a package-private `List<Override> overrides()` and make `Override` package-private (change `private record` → `record`, package-private) OR keep Override private and expose via a method. Since both classes are in `com.pathland.view`, package-private access works.

Actually cleanest: `Theme` gets `void emitInto(OpcodeSink sink)` already (it has it). `AdaptiveTheme.emitInto` needs the dark theme's paths to prefix them — so it can't just call `dark.emitInto(sink)` (that emits unprefixed). So AdaptiveTheme needs to iterate the dark theme's overrides with prefixing. So we need access to the raw (path, valueType, value) triples. I'll make `Theme.Override` package-private and add a package-private `List<Override> overrides()` accessor, or add a `Theme.forEach(ThemeVisitor)` method. Simplest: package-private `overrides()`.

Emitter: change `Emitter(OpcodeSink, Theme)` → accept AdaptiveTheme. But also keep Theme-only support? Let me offer the interface option or just AdaptiveTheme.

Actually — reconsider: the user's phrasing "have an AdaptiveTheme class that can be constructed from the 2?" strongly implies AdaptiveTheme is THE thing emitted. The demos would mount with AdaptiveTheme. A plain Theme (light-only) at mount is an edge case. I'll propose:
- `Emitter(OpcodeSink sink, AdaptiveTheme theme)` replaces `Emitter(OpcodeSink, Theme)` (the Theme-arg ctor was added this branch, unreleased). Demos + tests use AdaptiveTheme.
- `Theme` keeps `emit`/`emitInto` (still useful: light-only desktop ring, and as building blocks).

But wait — is dropping `Emitter(OpcodeSink, Theme)` too restrictive? A user with only a light theme would do `new AdaptiveTheme(light, new Theme())`. That's fine and explicit. I'll offer the choice though.

Let me also think: should AdaptiveTheme handle the case where light/dark have overlapping paths? Yes — they'll overlap by design (same token, different values). Both emitted; renderer's base map gets light, dark map gets dark. Good.

Now the questions:

Q1 (Emitter API): 
- A: Replace `Emitter(OpcodeSink, Theme)` with `Emitter(OpcodeSink, AdaptiveTheme)`; Theme keeps emit/emitInto as the building block. (Recommended — matches the model.)
- B: Keep both — introduce a tiny `ThemeEmitter` interface (`Theme` + `AdaptiveTheme` implement it), `Emitter(OpcodeSink, ThemeEmitter)`. More flexible but one more type.

Q2 (Rust mirror scope):
- A: Mirror fully — remove `dark_*` builders from `pathland_engine::Theme`, add `AdaptiveTheme { light, dark }` + `Engine::apply_adaptive_theme` (light + dark-prefixed). (Recommended for parity, matches previous "mirror in Rust" answer.)
- B: Java only for now.

Also minor: DemoTheme becomes light()/dark()/adaptive(); SessionApps + KitchenSinkViewTest use DemoTheme.adaptive(); EmitterTest updated; status.md files updated.

Let me also verify what other Java places reference `darkColor` — only DemoTheme + EmitterTest (confirmed). And the `Theme.emit` is used in the theme test + (if changed) nowhere else. And `new Emitter(sink, DemoTheme.theme())` in both SessionApps + KitchenSinkViewTest.

Let me present the plan and questions.

Both decisions locked in: the interface is **`ThemeData`**, and Rust gets mirrored. Final plan:

## Java

**`ThemeData.java` (new interface, `com.pathland.view`)**
```java
public interface ThemeData {
    void emitInto(OpcodeSink sink);                 // into an open frame
    default void emit(OpcodeSink sink) {            // one frame
        sink.beginFrame();
        emitInto(sink);
        sink.endFrame();
    }
}
```

**`Theme.java`** → `implements ThemeData`
- Keep single-value builders only: `color(path, argb)`, `f32`, `u32`, `u8`, `string`.
- **Remove** `darkColor`, `darkF32`, `darkString` and the `dark()` helper.
- `emitInto` pushes each override; make `Override` + an `overrides()` accessor package-private so `AdaptiveTheme` can read the dark theme's triples.

**`AdaptiveTheme.java` (new)**
```java
public final class AdaptiveTheme implements ThemeData {
    private final Theme light, dark;
    public AdaptiveTheme(Theme light, Theme dark) { ... }
    public void emitInto(OpcodeSink sink) {
        light.emitInto(sink);
        for (Override o : dark.overrides())
            sink.setDesignToken(dark(o.path()), o.valueType(), o.value());  // prefixes "dark."
    }
}
```
(Double-prefix guarded by the existing `startsWith("dark.")` check.)

**`Emitter.java`** — field + constructor param become `ThemeData`; `emitTheme()` calls `theme.emitInto(sink)` (unchanged behavior).

**Usages** — `DemoTheme` becomes `light()` / `dark()` / `adaptive()`; both `SessionApp`s and `KitchenSinkViewTest` use `new Emitter(sink, DemoTheme.adaptive())`.

**Tests** — rework `themeEmitsGlobalDesignTokenOverrides` to build light+dark `Theme`s + `AdaptiveTheme`, asserting base overrides emit unprefixed and dark ones as `dark.…`; add a `ThemeData`/light-only emit case.

## Rust (`pathland-engine`)

- `theme.rs`: **remove** `dark_color`, `dark_f32`, `dark_string`, `push_dark`. Add:
  ```rust
  #[derive(Debug, Clone, Default)]
  pub struct AdaptiveTheme { pub light: Theme, pub dark: Theme }
  impl AdaptiveTheme { pub fn new(light: Theme, dark: Theme) -> Self { ... } }
  ```
- `engine.rs`: extract `emit_token_override(path, &TokenValue, guest)` (shared Str-arena + numeric path); `apply_theme` uses it for light; new `apply_adaptive_theme(adaptive, guest)` emits light then dark-prefixed.
- Tests: `dark_builder_prepends_dark_prefix` becomes an `AdaptiveTheme` prefix test; add `apply_adaptive_theme` opcode assertions (paths `color.primary` + `dark.color.primary`).
- `pathland-view` (Rust) re-exports `AdaptiveTheme` alongside `Theme`.

## Docs/status
- `lib/java/pathland-view/status.md` — Theme bullet → `ThemeData` interface, single-value `Theme`, `AdaptiveTheme(light, dark)`.
- `pathland-engine/status.md` + `lib/rust/crates/pathland-view/status.md` — same model (Rust).

## Verification
- `cd lib/java && mvn -pl pathland-view -pl pathland-demo-views test`
- `cd lib/rust && cargo test -p pathland-engine -p pathland-view && ./check-wasm.sh`

